@node "pe_requirements" "Requirements"
@toc "pe_main"

  Wzonka-Lad Palette Editor requires

  - OS 3.0.
  - Wzonka.font, Zapot.font or Topaz.font.
  - A mouse. ;)

  If you want to edit Wzonka-Lad .prefs files, you need some of them
  previously saved with Wzonka-Lad. Note that Wzonka-Lad Palette
  Editor cannot create .prefs files from nothing as they contain more
  data than just the palette.

@endnode

@node "pe_compability" "Compability"
@toc "pe_main"

  Wzonka-Lad Palette Editor is compatible with all the versions of
  @{"Wzonka-Lad" link "wzonka"}. VGB compability is greatly extended to support almost
  all the colour values and options.

  Supported VGB .cfg colour tags:

  -c(0-3)          -bc(0-3)          -backgroundcolor(0-3)
                   -wc(0-3)          -windowcolor(0-3)
                   -sc(0-3)          -spritecolor(0-3)

  Supported VGB .cfg colour names:

  yellow, green, brown, black, cyan, red, white, pink, blue,
  orange, magenta, dkcyan, dkmagenta, dkcyan, dkgreen, dkred,
  dkblue, gray and dkgray.

  NOTE! I have no exact 24-bit values for gray and dkgray, so
        I had to use my imagination to add the support for those
        colours. If you have the true values for gray or dkgray
        (or something else, that's completely missing from the
        previous list), please send me a note.

  NOTE! -wc(0-3) and -windowcolor(0-3) are ignored if -c(0-3),
        -bc(0-3) or -backgroundcolor(0-3) tags are found. If not,
        then the backgroung colour is obtained from window colour
        tags.

  NOTE! As Wzonka-Lad doesn't support window colour editing, the
        background colours are outputted as window colours, too.

@endnode

@node "pe_palette" "Wzonka-Lad Palette Editor"
@toc "pe_main"

  People who are not interested in messing around with colour values
  can forget Wzonka-Lad Palette Editor, the emulator will work without
  it very nicely, indeed. But those who'd love to create wild colour
  schemes for different software to suit their tastes Wzonka-Lad Palette
  Editor will be the perfect tool.

  Forget unimaginative hex values. Wzonka-Lad Palette Editor will
  provide you with all the gadgets and example images required for
  fast and easy colour editing.

@endnode

@node "pe_features" "Features"
@toc "pe_main"

  Wzonka-Lad Palette Editor

  - Applies intelligent pen allocating/deallocating scheme to
    achieve the best results.
  - Allows you to select and edit the colours used by Wzonka-Lad.
  - Is 100% asm code.
  - Is free of any charge. ;) No keyfile is required to use Wzonka-Lad
    Palette Editor to the maximum.
  - Is fully multitasking.
  - Window is font sensitive.
  - Runs inside a window on Workbench screen.
  - Shows you the results in real time without even starting the
    emulator.
  - Supports Wzonka-Lad .prefs, VGB .cfg and Amiga .iff files.
  - Uses gadtools.library for the GUI.

@endnode

@node "history" "History"
@toc "main"

  v1.11.00 (19-Nov-2026):

    * AGA Full packs background tiles in groups of four and simplifies
      scanline sprite selection and placement
    * AGA Full writes each Game Boy window byte once per bitplane.
      Shifted windows read the background only at the two edges
    * AGA x/153 copies background and Game Boy window data two rows at a
      time, skips clean sprite rows in groups of four, and shares sprite
      merging across all flip combinations
    * AGA x/153 batches bulk background clears and stays within the
      allocated bitmap when the background or LCD is disabled
    * Both AGA refresh modes batch sprite-plane clears and merge sprite
      words in registers to reduce bitplane reads and writes
    * OS Screen and Scalable Window convert each nonempty sprite row with two
      lookups in a 64 KiB table. Both refresh modes retain live sprite palettes,
      transparency and background/sprite priority
    * OS Screen and Scalable Window share faster planar drawing. Full batches
      sprite clears, simplifies sprite selection, and writes each Game Boy
      window byte once per plane without spilling into the next row. x/153
      packs background tiles into aligned 32-bit writes, skips clean sprite
      rows in groups of four, and clears marked rows with batched stores
    * AGA Full, OS Screen and Scalable Window pack aligned Game Boy window
      pixels into 32-bit bitplane stores. OS Screen and Scalable Window x/153
      now stream window rows, reading the destination only at shifted edges
    * Scalable Window uses lookup tables, wider copy loops and repeat-row
      reuse to reduce scaling work. Native-width output skips horizontal
      scaling; 320x144 and 320x288 have dedicated routines
    * Misc settings includes optional Direct Window readback.
    * Number keys 1-9 resize the active Scalable Window's width; Shift+1-9
      sets its height
    * An optional EClock build profiles Window and AGA drawing and writes
      the results to T:Wzonka-GFX.txt on pause
    * Native Screen modes such as Lowres use planar output even when
      CyberGraphX is installed. OS screen speed limit also works on RTG screens
    * Noise uses a shift-register pattern. The normal loop is 32767
      steps and the short metallic mode is 127. The sample changes
      only when that mode changes
    * Wave samples use the whole 4-bit range, so a value of 15
      reaches full scale
    * Master-volume (NR50) writes no longer overwrite the volume with channel
      routing (NR51), fixing music fade-out hangs
    * All sound modes apply the separate left/right NR50 levels and correct
      NR51 stereo routing. NR50 level zero is quiet, not completely muted
    * Pulse and wave playback rates retain fractional-tone precision without
      18-bit wrapping. Noise uses the correct LFSR clock rate; NR43 shifts 14
      and 15 stop the noise clock
    * Prevent Amiga lockups with AHI: pause ultrasonic playback rates >=
      1048576 Hz that overflow its mixer. Game Boy sound registers,
      channel status and timers are unaffected
    * y Hz mode tracks triggers, DAC power, length timers and NR52 channel
      status without making AHI calls between its selected output updates
    * Sound-register writes cannot revive a channel stopped by its DAC,
      length timer or sweep. Envelope writes no longer restart the envelope
    * High mode checks sweep overflow on trigger without changing pitch early,
      and checks overflow again after each frequency update
    * Pulse-duty changes preserve the emulated CPU flags. Restarting audio
      resets sample caches and restores active note volumes from registers
    * Powering off sound also clears master routing, volume and internal pitch
    * FULL CPU mode writes stack pairs directly in high RAM and work RAM,
      preserving echo RAM and using byte handlers at I/O and memory boundaries
    * Interrupt dispatch masks IF with IE before choosing the highest-priority
      request, including the EI and RETI paths
    * HALT resumes when an enabled interrupt becomes pending even with IME off,
      without servicing that interrupt. STOP is left halted
    * EI enables interrupts after the following instruction in all CPU modes.
      DI cancels a pending EI, and the following instruction's cycles are
      counted. EI/HALT and EI/RETI preserve the correct interrupt state
    * The HALT bug's suppressed PC increment survives a CPU sweep boundary,
      including debug builds
    * ADD SP,x and LD HL,SP+x set C from the unsigned low-byte addition.
      The full core also sets H from bit 3 and clears N and Z
    * ADD HL,HL stores a literal H flag in the full core, rather than a
      deferred flag based on stale operands
    * CCF clears H and N in the full core while preserving Z and complementing C
    * DAA handles every A/N/H/C input combination in the full core, preserves
      N, clears H, and sets Z from the adjusted accumulator
    * Corrected cycle counts in both cores for JR, JP, CALL, RET, RETI, RST
      and BIT n,(HL), including taken and untaken conditional paths

  v1.10.00 (16-Nov-2026)
    * DAA sets Z from the adjusted accumulator
    * ADD HL,BC / ADD HL,DE / ADD HL,SP set H from the carry out of bit 11
    * CCF sets H from the old carry, then complements C
    * HALT with interrupts disabled waits. The halt bug runs only when an
      enabled interrupt is already pending. A halted CPU resumes, without
      servicing the interrupt, when one becomes pending while interrupts
	  stay disabled. STOP is left halted
    * The crippled core has the same HALT fix. It does not emulate DAA or
      the H flag. Its HALT debug conditional is closed, matching the full core
    * A missing startup library or key file is reported on the console
      before exit
    * DMG OAM corruption is assembled only when GAMEBOY_OAM_BUG is 1. It
      defaults to 0, so inc/dec and ld (hl+/-) do not alter other OAM entries
    * ADC and SBC set Z when the result byte is 0. The carry path presets
      Z as well as X, because ADDX and SUBX leave Z unchanged on zero. The
      crippled core has the same fix
    * Line-by-line sprite drawing walks OAM from sprite 0 and draws higher
      indices first, so sprite 0 is in front. Every sprite on the line is drawn
    * Channel 1 sweep runs again. Its new frequency is converted like a
      square write, and the channel goes silent past 2047
    * A wave high-byte write uses the same divider as the low byte, so the
      note stays in tune
    * Noise plays at the LFSR rate. Its envelope arms the noise channel.
      A trigger with the length bit set starts the note
    * A rising envelope stops at volume 15 and holds there
    * Length counters clock at 256 Hz. Squares and noise count 64 steps,
      the wave counts 256, and the channel goes silent at zero
    * NR52 bits 0-3 follow the running channels. A trigger sets the bit
      when the DAC is on. The bit clears when the length expires, the
      DAC turns off, channel 1 sweep overflows, or the APU powers off.
      Writing NR52 while the APU is already on keeps those bits, so a room
      change can silence what is playing
    * Powering the APU off clears envelope and sweep state, so the channel
      stays silent
    * Sprite drawing optimizations
    * Window mode obeys now "OS screen speed limit" setting)

  v1.01.00 (14-Aug-1999)
    * Wzonka-Lad doesn't check the ID area in a ROM dump file,
      so you can run all the software you want with Wzonka-Lad.

  v1.00.00 (25-May-1999)
    * ROM+MBC5+RUMBLE(+SRAM+BATTERY)-mappers are now emulated,
      but the rumble device emulation is not implemented.
    * Optimized scanline emulation loop.
    * MBC3's clock I/O was emulated, but the clock itself wasn't.
      Now it is.
    * Enhanced sound channel 4 emulation.
    * When the Game Boy screen is turned off all the data is
      cleared instead of doing nothing in AGA mode.
    * Palette emulation came one frame too early in AGA modes.
    * When the size of the battery RAM was bigger than 8kb the
      active bank wasn't saved.
    * Main menu window functions can now be accessed with keyboard.
    * Interleaved $xx and $CBxx optcode routines in FULL CPU
      emulation, like it was done in FAST earlier, resulting in
      a smaller executable.
    * Enhanced Info-window data display.
    * Wzonka-Lad can now only be used in testing unlicenced
      software (ie. the programmes you and your friends have
      written). Read the documentation for further information.

  [SOME RELEASES MIGHT BE MISSING FROM THIS SLOT]
  
  v0.99.12 (29-Nov-1998)
  FULL executable now emulates the OAM RAM bug. Now the DMA transfer doesn't
  transfer all the bits as 4bits / sprite are unused, which is the way the
  real GB DMA works. This slows down the DMA transfer a bit, but should
  offer a better compability. Sound data registers (for channels 1, 2, 3 and 4)
  are now cleared when the "All Sound OFF" flag is set to 0, and writes to
  them are cancelled until the flag is set to 1. Fixed few instruction timings.
  Optimized CPU emulation a little. Up to 64Mbit ROM and 1Mbit RAM sizes are
  supported. When saving battery RAM, the "/" symbol in cartridge name isn't
  converted to "\" anymore, as it didn't work. Now it's replaced with "-", and
  $80 is converted to " ". Fixed few hardware register emulations. GB Z80 CPU
  core executes now 456 cycles per scanline. Fixed few hardware register
  emulations. Enhanced interrupt emulation (big thanks to Noca$h!). TIMER IRQ
  emulation is now four times as accurate, but this slows down the emulation.
  At least Trax and Boxxle 2 work now. The input from the user is now checked
  three times as often. Optimized JOYPAD, KEYBOARD, SEGA 2B, SEGA 4B, JOY 1B
  and JOY 2B input routines. All MBC3 variants are now recognized and emulated
  except for the MBC3+TIMER(+RAM+BATTERY) one, as I don't know about the timer
  (bugfixed MBC3 emulation as well). MBC3's RTC feature is only emulated
  read-wise as I don't know how the writes to the RTC registers are done.
  MBC4 and MBC5 mappers are recognized, but only ROM+MBC5(+RAM+BATTERY) is
  emulated (more info needed!). Added more ROM manufacturer ids! Fixed a bug
  in the AGA SCREEN MODE which could have made Amiga sprite garbage appear on
  screen when the GB's hardware window was out of screen and enabled (Pokemon).
  Fixed DI+HALT emulation. Few interrupt related special cases are now
  emulated. Changed the example picture in visual settings window in Wzonka-Lad
  and Palette Editor main window. Now recognizes correctly GB, SGB and CGB
  cartridges. ROM sizes of 9Mbit, 10Mbit and 12Mbit are now supported
  (but not tested). There are two versions of Wzonka-Lad, R for registered
  users and E for the rest. R can only be recieved by emailing me.

  v0.99.11 (15-Jul-1998)
  Mouse pointer is now busy in all Wzonka-Lad's windows when the emulation
  is running. There are three different archives of v0.99.11, one compiled
